{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":48894,"items":[{"abstract":"<jats:p>Noisy social text can be transformed into semantic representations, but evaluating the resulting artifacts is difficult when source records, ontology activations, graph resources, conformance reports, and model inputs are considered separately. The evaluation problem is whether their connections remain inspectable as these artifacts are generated and used. This study examines the above continuity using 1614 Reddit posts about artificial intelligence and technology. Ontology activations produce sibling Resource Description Framework (RDF) and numeric representations linked through stable identifiers. Explicit correspondence checks and controlled fault tests examine these connections, while Shapes Constraint Language (SHACL) checks assess conformance to declared graph constraints. Existing verification checks detect 15 of 22 injected continuity faults. The remaining seven are detected only by supplementary diagnostics. Separate human assessment of 120 validation texts evaluates concept presence and local polarity against the source text, distinguishing extraction quality from artifact continuity. Downstream classification provides secondary evidence of modest mean macro-F1 gains in agreement with inherited lexical labels, varying by model across three seeds on one fixed split. The contribution is an evaluation of governed semantic artifact continuity at the post and variable levels, making the connections among these artifacts and their failure conditions clear and inspectable.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/info17100998","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,10]],"date-time":"2026-10-10T03:18:57Z","timestamp":1791602337000},"title":["Evaluating Semantic Artifact Continuity for Noisy Social Text: RDF Materialization, SHACL Conformance, and Model Input Evidence"],"author":[{"given":"Ricardo","family":"Flores-Moyano","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Departamento de Ingenier\u00eda en Ciencias de la Computaci\u00f3n, Universidad San Francisco de Quito, Quito 170901, Ecuador"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-3575-4493","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Felipe","family":"Rosero-Polo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Departamento de Ingenier\u00eda en Ciencias de la Computaci\u00f3n, Universidad San Francisco de Quito, Quito 170901, Ecuador"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jos\u00e9","family":"Vega-S\u00e1nchez","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Departamento de Ingenier\u00eda en Ciencias de la Computaci\u00f3n, Universidad San Francisco de Quito, Quito 170901, Ecuador"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9379-8151","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Maria","family":"Baldeon-Calisto","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Business, Wake Forest University, Winston-Salem, NC 27109, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2078-2489\/17\/10\/998\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/info17100998","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]]}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-20","title":["Supplemental Information 20: Training and validation CTC loss curves along with some examples of text extraction of the CRNN model."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-20","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T08:12:34Z","timestamp":1791879154000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.18653\/2026.wnut-1.1","title":["A Precision-First Whitespace Restoration Pipeline for Noisy Urdu Tafseer Text"],"author":[{"given":"Muhammad Mubashir","family":"Hassan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/2026.wnut-1.1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,29]],"date-time":"2026-10-29T16:16:38Z","timestamp":1793290598000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Automatic speech recognition (ASR) has been increasingly adopted for generating transcriptions to train text-to-speech (TTS) models, substantially reducing manual annotation costs. However, transcription errors introduced by ASR systems inevitably degrade TTS performance, and existing approaches lack explicit quality awareness, leaving it unclear whether TTS models can learn to distinguish and respond to varying transcription quality. This study investigates whether explicit quality supervision can enable TTS models to develop quality-aware representations and achieve controllable stable synthesis. This study proposes transcription quality labels (TQL), automatically derived from connectionist temporal classification scores. During training, TQL provides explicit quality supervision, enabling the model to associate transcription quality with acoustic characteristics. During inference, setting TQL to high values guides the model toward stable synthesis that faithfully follows the input text. Under controlled conditions isolating the effect of transcription quality, our best TQL-based model achieves 8.4% word error rate compared to 15.9% for the baseline, a 47% relative reduction. While synthesis accuracy gains are significant, naturalness improvements are modest (mean opinion score of 3.66 vs. 3.55), indicating that TQL primarily enhances synthesis stability rather than perceptual quality. Further analysis confirms that synthesis quality varies systematically with TQL values at inference, demonstrating learned quality-conditioned behavior.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1108\/atsip-02-2026-0015","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,3]],"date-time":"2026-09-03T11:43:22Z","timestamp":1788435802000},"title":["CTC score-based transcription quality annotation for stable text-to-speech synthesis training on noisy transcriptions"],"author":[{"given":"Jingyi","family":"Feng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Nagoya University , Nagoya,","place":["Japan"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yusuke","family":"Yasuda","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nagoya University Department of Informatics, , Nagoya,","place":["Japan"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tomoki","family":"Toda","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nagoya University , Nagoya,","place":["Japan"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.emerald.com\/atsip\/article-pdf\/15\/1\/532\/11820606\/atsip-02-2026-0015en.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"syndication"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.emerald.com\/atsip\/article-pdf\/15\/1\/532\/11820606\/atsip-02-2026-0015en.pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1108\/atsip-02-2026-0015","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7445919","title":["A Rule-Based Text Mining and Information Extraction Framework for Indian Parliamentary Question-Answer Documents,A Pilot Study on Lok Sabha Records"],"author":[{"given":"Sivakumar","family":"S","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Legislative question-answer (Q&amp;A) records published by the Parliament of India on the Sansad and eParlib portals constitute a large, continuously growing, and largely unstructured repository of official government responses covering health, infrastructure, finance, and social welfare policy. Manually locating, comparing, and quantifying information across these documents is time-consuming and error-prone. This paper proposes a five-layer rule-based architecture - Ingestion, Normalization, Extraction, Analysis, and Output - for converting Lok Sabha Starred and Unstarred Question PDF documents into structured, analyzable data. The architecture is demonstrated on a pilot corpus of ten Lok Sabha Q&amp;A documents drawn directly from the Sansad and eParlib portals, spanning eight ministries (Health and Family Welfare, Home Affairs, Women and Child Development, Jal Shakti, Road Transport and Highways, Corporate Affairs, Earth Sciences, and Finance). The pipeline performs automatic metadata parsing, rule-based extraction of government scheme acronyms, detection of embedded state-wise and year-wise statistical tables, and lexical frequency analysis. On this pilot corpus, the system correctly parsed metadata for all ten documents, identified structured numeric tables in five of them, and extracted 15 distinct scheme\/programme acronyms with no manual correction. Two case studies -\u00a0state-wise heatstroke mortality data and the National Family Health Survey (NFHS) child-stunting trend (1992-2024) - illustrate the framework&amp;apos;s ability to recover time-series and cross-state statistics directly from free-text answers. The results indicate that a lightweight, fully auditable rule-based approach can reliably structure short-form government Q&amp;A text without requiring large annotated training sets, and can serve as a preprocessing stage for higher-level classification or retrieval systems.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7445919","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,17]],"date-time":"2026-09-17T15:33:43Z","timestamp":1789659223000}},{"DOI":"10.54254\/2755-2721\/2026.36442","title":["Research on Text Information Extraction and Imbalanced Classification Methods for Enterprise Profiling"],"author":[{"given":"Xinyi","family":"Xu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Tongji University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>This research focuses on enterprise profiling in scenarios where large volumes of diverse texts\u2014such as registration records, annual reports, news articles, and bidding notices\u2014are continuously generated. Instead of relying solely on a single data representation or classification model, we developed a comprehensive natural language processing (NLP) pipeline for extracting key information and identifying industries. The pipeline consists of several steps. First, we use a BERT-BiLSTM-CRF model to identify core e\/nterprise entities. Then, we combine TF-IDF with BERT embeddings to create a hybrid feature scheme that captures both lexical cues and contextual semantics. To address the challenge of imbalanced industry labels, we apply SMOTE in the dense semantic space and pair it with Focal Loss to enhance learning for minority classes. Additionally, we introduce a Stacking strategy to integrate outputs from different models, making predictions more stable. Tests on a self-compiled dataset covering ten national economic sectors and about 50,000 enterprises show that our method achieves a macro-F1 score of 95.4%. It outperforms traditional machine learning baselines and single deep learning models, offering more reliable recognition for minority classes. These results suggest that our framework is well-suited for applications such as supply chain partner discovery, industrial mapping, and targeted investment promotion.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.54254\/2755-2721\/2026.36442","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]],"date-time":"2026-09-01T03:03:32Z","timestamp":1788231812000}},{"DOI":"10.18653\/2026.wnut-1.3","title":["Beyond Clean Text: Evaluating Encoder and Decoder Robustness for Bangla Event Detection in Noisy Text"],"author":[{"given":"Tanvir Ahmed","family":"Sijan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"S M Golam","family":"Rifat","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nayeemul","family":"Islam","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Md Musfique","family":"Anwar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/2026.wnut-1.3","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,29]],"date-time":"2026-10-29T16:16:38Z","timestamp":1793290598000}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-10","title":["Supplemental Information 10: End-to-end pipeline: from the input image through component and text detection, OCR, node extraction, and component-to-node mapping to netlist generation and circuit solutions."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103\/supp-10","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]],"date-time":"2026-10-13T08:12:34Z","timestamp":1791879154000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4103","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Databases of thermoelectric properties are built largely by extracting values from the literature, and the choice of extraction method determines how many records are recovered and how many are wrong. We compare five methods on the same paragraph-level task: a corpus-informed regular-expression baseline, the published ChemDataExtractor thermoelectric parsers, a MaterialsBERT-based named-entity model with rule-based linking, a single-prompt large language model (LLM) and an LLM agent. The methods were developed on a 100-paragraph development sample (the rules of the regular-expression baseline earlier, on the whole corpus), frozen, and run on a 200-paragraph test sample drawn by stratified sampling from 311 papers (mostly arXiv preprints), with a paper-level split between the two samples. A record counts as correct only if property, value, temperature and material all match the gold standard. On the 160 in-scope test paragraphs (241 core records), the single-prompt LLM reached F1 = 0.79 (95 % CI 0.71\u20130.87) and the agent 0.73 (0.64\u20130.83), against 0.22 for the regular-expression baseline and 0.06 for the ChemDataExtractor and NER pipelines. Both LLM methods recover property, value and temperature with F1 above 0.94; most of their remaining losses arise from the material criterion; in most of these cases one material name contains the other. We found no evidence of an accuracy improvement from the tested agent configuration, which differs from the single prompt in several components at once. We release the protocol, code, gold standard and all method outputs in a public repository.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.26434\/chemrxiv.15010913\/v1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,23]],"date-time":"2026-10-23T04:55:37Z","timestamp":1792731337000},"title":["Benchmarking thermoelectric property extraction from scientific text"],"author":[{"given":"Berna","family":"Uyan\u0131k","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/chemrxiv.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.26434\/chemrxiv.15010913\/v1","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26434\/chemrxiv.15010913\/v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,22]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>We compare five methods for extracting thermoelectric property records from paragraphs of the literature (regular expressions, the ChemDataExtractor thermoelectric parsers, MaterialsBERT named-entity recognition with rule-based linking, a single-prompt large language model (LLM) and an LLM agent) on one corpus of 311 papers with one scoring rule, with all methods frozen before the 200-paragraph test sample was annotated. A record counts as correct only if property, value, temperature and material all match the gold standard. On the 160 in-scope test paragraphs (241 core records), the single-prompt LLM reached F1 = 0.79 (95% CI 0.71\u20130.87) and the agent 0.73 (0.64\u20130.83), against 0.22 for the regular-expression baseline and 0.06 for the ChemDataExtractor and NER pipelines; both LLM methods recover property, value and temperature with F1 above 0.94, and most of their remaining errors concern the material. We found no evidence of an accuracy improvement from the tested agent configuration, and we release the protocol, code, gold standard and all method outputs.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.26434\/chemrxiv.15010913\/v2","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,29]],"date-time":"2026-10-29T09:13:52Z","timestamp":1793265232000},"title":["Benchmarking thermoelectric property extraction from scientific text"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3934-5538","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Berna","family":"Uyan\u0131k","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/chemrxiv.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.26434\/chemrxiv.15010913\/v2","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26434\/chemrxiv.15010913\/v2","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,29]]}},{"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) achieve strong performance in multimodal information extraction (MIE). In healthcare and elderly-care applications, restricted data access, privacy constraints, and scarce domain-specific annotations often favor inference with frozen models. Standard evaluation measures whether extracted results match reference labels but does not test whether a result changes when a text span identified by the model as its support is modified.\nControlled Text-Anchored Evidence-Dependence Verification for Low-Resource Multimodal Information Extraction (CEDV-MIE) implements this test as a bounded inference-time procedure. A text span reported as support for an initial schema-constrained extraction is modified under validity constraints, while the paired image, visual contextual records, and non-target text remain fixed. The original and perturbed extractions are compared to produce a prediction-level consistency record and a final schema-constrained extraction. No parameter updates or additional evidence annotations are required.\nAcross four public MIE benchmarks and four frozen backbones, CEDV-MIE improves average F\u2081 over direct prompting in all 16 dataset\u2013backbone configurations, with a mean gain of 3.85 points (range: 1.48\u201310.78). Against a self-consistency control using seven chain-of-thought samples per input (SC-7), CEDV-MIE achieves higher average F\u2081 in 14 of 16 configurations under the same maximum allowance of seven backbone calls. The post-hoc Consistency Check Score (CCS) increases in all eight diagnostic configurations evaluated with two backbones (mean +0.195); evidence-stability accuracy and F\u2081 also increase on the validated diagnostic subset.<\/p>","DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11232338\/v1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,5]],"date-time":"2026-10-05T08:04:06Z","timestamp":1791187446000},"title":["CEDV-MIE: Controlled Text-Anchored Evidence-Dependence Verification for Low-Resource Multimodal Information Extraction"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0424-629X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiaoming","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhongyuan University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yinghui","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhongyuan University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Guan","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhongyuan University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Naveed","family":"Iqbal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"King Fahd University of Petroleum and Minerals"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"family":"XuepingPeng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Technology Sydney"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jie","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"North China University of Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Long","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Durham University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11232338\/v1","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11232338\/v1.html","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11232338\/v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,5]]}},{"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong capabilities in document key\u2013value pair(KVP) extraction, yet controlled evaluations of their robustness to optical character recognition (OCR)output remain limited. This leaves an important gap in understanding their reliability within real-world OCR-to-LLM pipelines. Unlike end-to-end Vision-Language Models (VLMs) that conflate visualperception with semantic extraction, modular pipelines allow errors to be explicitly isolated andaudited. To bridge this evaluation gap, we introduce a controlled, large-scale benchmarking frameworkdesigned to distinguish downstream LLM extraction behavior from upstream OCR degradation. Thisbenchmark evaluates 136 experimental configurations and 17,688 deterministic document-level inferenceinstances across five instruction-tuned open-weight LLMs (2B\u20138B parameters), three complementarydatasets, and four text-quality conditions. This extensive matrix pairs a full zero-shot comparisonand targeted one- to three-shot experiments with a confirmatory sensitivity analysis comprising 40configurations across 20 frozen demonstration sets. By separating Key Recall (annotated-field recovery)from Exact Match and Value F1 (exact and partial value recovery, respectively), we reveal whetherOCR degradation affects field identification and value reproduction differently across models. Crucially,our findings challenge three practical evaluation assumptions: (1) that clean-text performance is areliable proxy for real-world robustness, (2) that model rankings remain consistent across annotation-derived Gold and OCR-derived text conditions, and (3) that providing few-shot demonstrationsmonotonically improves extraction accuracy. By exposing vulnerabilities such as model-rankingreversals and unstable few-shot gains, this benchmark provides a rigorous and reproducible frameworkfor evaluating LLM reliability under noisy document conditions. To facilitate reproducible research,our entire benchmarking framework, dataset splits, and evaluation scripts are open-sourced.<\/p>","DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11199953\/v1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,5]],"date-time":"2026-10-05T06:35:06Z","timestamp":1791182106000},"title":["From Pixels to Pairs: A Comprehensive Benchmark of LLM-Driven Key\u2013Value Extraction in Noisy Document Settings"],"author":[{"given":"Zahra","family":"Anvari","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Westcliff University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11199953\/v1","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11199953\/v1.html","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11199953\/v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,5]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7588878","title":["Leverage and Structural Information in Noisy Stochastic-volatility Prices"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-1918-5458","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Joan","family":"Vidal Llaurado","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Leverage can preserve information about a hidden volatility drift as covariance shrinks, provided its response propagates through the latent dynamics. For a bivariate Ornstein\u2013Uhlenbeck model observed through noisy log-price levels on a fixed grid, we prove exact identification at positive covariance and adaptive squared drift risk of order min{1, [n(s\u00b2 + se\u00b2)]\u207b\u00b9}, where n counts observation blocks, s is the covariance scale and e the leverage amplitude. Matching lower bounds on specified drift neighborhoods use the full price law. Dynamically exciting leverage thus permits learning where zero leverage prevents uniform consistency. Nuisance parameters are unknown; confidence coverage includes zero covariance and absent excitation. Precision requires fixed excitation and residual covariance margins, fixed positive level noise, a nonaliased drift branch and a sufficiently small, unevaluated covariance ceiling.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7588878","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,10]],"date-time":"2026-10-10T06:48:47Z","timestamp":1791614927000}},{"DOI":"10.64643\/ijirt.208465-459","title":["Robustness Analysis Of Lightweight Machine Learning Models For Sentiment Classification Under Noisy Text"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.64643\/ijirt.208465-459","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,15]],"date-time":"2026-09-15T05:40:16Z","timestamp":1789450816000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/cisat71030.2026.11682145","title":["Reproducible Dual-Path NLP for Character Graph Extraction and Context-Aware State Tracking in Low-Resource Short Text"],"author":[{"given":"Yizhuo","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Qilu Institute of Technology, China","place":["Qufu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/cisat71030.2026.11682145","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,10]],"date-time":"2026-09-10T19:10:05Z","timestamp":1789067405000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11682029\/11682043\/11682145.pdf?arnumber=11682145","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/ijcnn69854.2026.11728883","title":["Budgeted Cascaded Routing for LLM-Based Structured Extraction from Noisy Conversations"],"author":[{"given":"Tianzhe","family":"Ning","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Donghua University","place":["Shanghai, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Guohua","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Donghua University","place":["Shanghai, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/ijcnn69854.2026.11728883","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,19]],"date-time":"2026-10-19T19:18:04Z","timestamp":1792437484000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/11728400\/11728402\/11728883.pdf?arnumber=11728883","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.3724\/2096-7004.di.2026.0761","title":["CACC: Class-Aware Consistency Cleaning for Long-Tailed Noisy Text Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Kaibin","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ge","family":"Lan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yufei","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zongyu","family":"Bai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuzhi","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3724\/2096-7004.di.2026.0761","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,19]],"date-time":"2026-10-19T09:12:15Z","timestamp":1792401135000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.sciengine.com\/sci-open\/api\/v1\/open\/file\/pdf\/FF2535C2208C404D9817BF6E49084F98","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.sciengine.com\/doi\/10.3724\/2096-7004.di.2026.0761","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.sciengine.com\/sci-open\/api\/v1\/open\/file\/pdf\/FF2535C2208C404D9817BF6E49084F98","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.66977\/xsci.2610.000g","title":["Systematic Review as Recipe Book for Nuanced Emotion Detection in Noisy Text"],"author":[{"given":"Jeff","family":"Winchell","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jamila","family":"Iqbal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nwagu Chima","family":"Ajanwachuku","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Moody","family":"Farra","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mostafa","family":"Galal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alonzo","family":"Machiraju","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Recognizing emotion in informal text at the granularity mental health support requires, telling nervousness from fear or remorse from sadness, remains far below usable accuracy: five years of work on Google\u2019s GoEmotions multi-label 28-emotion dataset has reached only .5475 macro F1, and no systematic review has examined why. Accurate emotion recognition is an important predictor of mental healthcare success, yet its subjectivity makes it difficult for even trained human annotators to agree on nuanced emotions in noisy text. Our systematic search identified 494 papers. Inclusion required the full 28-emotion dataset, deep learning models, and reported macro F1, yielding 30 papers and 71 models. Among the highest-scoring models, 9 of the top 10 employ explicit emotion-learning architectures or objective functions. We found no LLM-based models exceeding the foundation paper\u2019s baseline performance. Only 5 of the 71 models underwent extensive hyperparameter tuning. We attributed the observed ceiling to tuning practice as much as architectural limits. Systematic reviews are rare in AI\/ML relative to medicine. This scarcity, combined with a submission surge that has outpaced peer-review capacity, plausibly explains why the field\u2019s true state-of-the-art result went unrecognized for years. This recipe book details the emotion-specific design and tuning techniques associated with the strongest results. The strongest results in the corpus are associated with combining explicit emotion-learning architectures, objective functions, and rigorous tuning, though the reviewed models remain well short of the performance likely needed for practical usability in mental healthcare. Current evidence does not support LLMs as a competitive alternative.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.66977\/xsci.2610.000g","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,4]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,4]],"date-time":"2026-10-04T14:02:13Z","timestamp":1791122533000}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3801438.3806612","title":["A Transformer-Based Framework for Russian Text Classification and Information Extraction"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-1447-6734","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dandan","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Heilongjiang University of Technology, Jixi, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4288-1566","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yang","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Heilongjiang University of Technology, Jixi, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-2926-4804","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hongling","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Heilongjiang University of Technology, Jixi, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-2974-7613","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lina","family":"Hou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Heilongjiang University of Technology, Jixi, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-2997-3628","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiuru","family":"Deng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Heilongjiang University of Technology, Jixi, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3801438.3806612","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,24]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]],"date-time":"2026-09-04T15:20:13Z","timestamp":1788535213000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3801438.3806612","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.18653\/2026.findings-emnlp.1901","title":["GRIP: Gated Routing of Instance-level Preferences for Noisy Long-Tailed Text Classification"],"author":[{"given":"Ziqing","family":"Tian","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Bo","family":"Yuan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/2026.findings-emnlp.1901","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,29]],"date-time":"2026-10-29T16:16:22Z","timestamp":1793290582000}},{"DOI":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4106\/supp-1","title":["Supplemental Information 1: Feature Extraction."],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7717\/peerj-cs.4106\/supp-1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T08:15:20Z","timestamp":1791792920000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.7717\/peerj-cs.4106","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Handwritten prescription recognition in low-resource healthcare depends not only on the accuracy of the recognition network but also on the quality of the image presented to that network. This study implemented and evaluated a preprocessing and segmentation pipeline designed to convert noisy, variably illuminated and geometrically inconsistent prescription images into standardised inputs suitable for handwritten-text recognition. The workflow was grounded in a quantitative experimental design using the Doctor's Handwritten Prescription BD dataset, comprising 4,680 segmented pharmaceutical-word images and a 60%\/20%\/20% train-validation-test partition. The pipeline combined grayscale conversion, adaptive thresholding and binarization, Hough-transform-based skew correction, morphological denoising, aspect-ratio-preserving resizing and a final segmentation confirmation stage intended for line localization on unsegmented prescription pages. Segmentation performance was evaluated using Intersection over Union (IoU), with a pre-specified acceptance criterion of 0.90 or higher. The source dissertation states that segmentation success was confirmed at or above this threshold, but it does not separately tabulate an exact IoU point estimate; this article therefore reports the threshold-level result without inventing additional precision. The pipeline is technically important because each stage targets a distinct source of variance that can degrade downstream feature extraction: illumination, background noise, rotation, scale and boundary uncertainty. The study further incorporated contextual augmentation to reduce the mismatch between the Bangladesh-origin source corpus and the intended South Sudanese application environment. The results support the preprocessing and localization design as a necessary front end for the Residual U-Net recognition system, while also showing that external validity remains limited by the absence of a fully local prescription corpus. A clinically credible next step is prospective validation using authentic South Sudanese prescription images, with explicit reporting of IoU distributions, failure cases and computational cost on low-resource hardware.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.68258\/pajps_tox.63833","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,16]],"date-time":"2026-10-16T06:47:08Z","timestamp":1792133228000},"title":["From Noisy Prescription Images to Reliable Text-Line Localization: Preprocessing and Segmentation for Handwritten Medical OCR in South Sudan"],"author":[{"given":"Aguet Elizabeth","family":"Deng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/parj.africa\/pajps_tox\/article\/download\/article-35222\/pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/parj.africa\/pajps_tox\/article\/download\/article-35222\/docx","content-type":"application\/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/parj.africa\/pajps_tox\/article\/download\/article-35222\/cover-image","content-type":"image\/png","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/parj.africa\/pajps_tox\/article\/download\/article-35222\/html","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/parj.africa\/pajps_tox\/article\/download\/article-35222\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.68258\/pajps_tox.63833","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]]}},{"DOI":"10.15407\/spqeo29.03.344","title":["Improving accuracy of differential parameter extraction from noisy measurement data"],"author":[{"given":"D.A.","family":"Kuznietsova","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"V. Lashkaryov Institute of Semiconductor Physics of the National Academy of Sciences of Ukraine, 41 Nauki Avenue, 03028 Kyiv, Ukraine"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>The paper addresses the problem of enhancing the accuracy of differential analysis of integral characteristics of semiconductor structures under conditions of experimental noise. It is shown that standard measurement methods with a linear voltage step lead to significant errors in calculating the dimensionless sensitivity, manifesting themselves as non-physical oscillations that complicate the identification of charge transport mechanisms. A comparative analysis of three data processing methods is conducted: the optimal step method, polynomial approximation (Savitzky\u2013Golay filter), and adaptive filtering based on the Kalman algorithm. It is established that application of the optimal step method based on logarithmic current sampling avoids differentiation artifacts without introducing phase delays characteristic of smoothing methods. The proposed approaches are tested on experimental current-voltage characteristics of OLED structures, confirming their suitability for precise diagnostics of electrophysical parameters of semiconductor devices.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.15407\/spqeo29.03.344","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,29]],"date-time":"2026-09-29T06:30:30Z","timestamp":1790663430000}},{"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:p>Patient-specific phenotype information extraction identifiers and normalizes phenotype entities and links them with individual patients, supporting rare disease research and genotype-phenotype analysis. However, existing datasets rarely combine full-text biomedical content with detailed annotations of patients, phenotypes, related entities, and their associations. In this work, we present PatientPheX, a corpus for patient-specific phenotype information extraction comprising 200 full-text articles, primarily case reports and case series. The corpus provides mention annotations and normalization for seven biomedical entity types, together with explicit associations between patients and entities across sentences, paragraphs, and sections. Negated findings and composite phenotype mentions are also annotated. Corpus construction combines automated pre-annotation, large language model (LLM)-assisted verification, and manual review and adjudication. Benchmarks cover entity recognition and normalization and patient\u2013phenotype association extraction using gold-standard and predicted entities. The overall document-level entity F1 was 86.95%, while phenotype F1 was 79.33%. The best relation extraction macro-F1 decreased from 84.14% with gold-standard entities to 54.98% with predicted entities, highlighting the combined challenges of phenotype extraction and patient attribution. PatientPheX provides a resource for developing and evaluating patient-centered information extraction methods and supports the construction of structured patient-level knowledge resources for phenotype-driven biomedical research.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.64898\/2026.10.07.757319","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]],"date-time":"2026-10-15T08:55:10Z","timestamp":1792054510000},"title":["PatientPheX: A Full-Text Biomedical Corpus for Patient-Specific Phenotype Information Extraction"],"author":[{"given":"Xuanlyu","family":"Zhu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Technology, Dalian University of Technology, Dalian 116024, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5141-0259","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ling","family":"Luo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Technology, Dalian University of Technology, Dalian 116024, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhijun","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Technology, Dalian University of Technology, Dalian 116024, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chentao","family":"Huang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Technology, Dalian University of Technology, Dalian 116024, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yunxiang","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Technology, Dalian University of Technology, Dalian 116024, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yang","family":"Zhong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Technology, Dalian University of Technology, Dalian 116024, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tengxiao","family":"Lv","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Technology, Dalian University of Technology, Dalian 116024, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Juntao","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation